eyebrow: Guide · Materials
h1: Stock control for solar installers, in practical steps.
lead: Solar jobs fail on the day a panel, inverter or cable is missing. Stock control is the habit of knowing what you hold, where it is and which job took it, and it needs only a few disciplined steps.
answer: Keep one catalogue, define each store, record every movement against a reason and a job, set reorder levels, and reserve stock for approved jobs so two teams do not count the same item.
published: 2026-10-11
sections:
  - h: Clean the catalogue first
    p:
      - Duplicate items with slightly different names make every count unreliable. Give each item one entry with its category, brand, specification, unit and tax rate.
  - h: Define every place stock can sit
    p:
      - Main store, site store, vehicle, vendor-held and returned stock are different places. Give each a name so a movement always has a clear from and to.
  - h: Record every movement with a reason
    p:
      - Stock moves for a few reasons, and each one should be recorded when it happens, not at month end.
    list:
      - Purchase received
      - Transfer between stores
      - Issue to a project
      - Return from a project
      - Damage, loss or adjustment after a count
  - h: Tie issues to jobs
    p:
      - When material leaves the store for a job, record the job. Over time you can compare what each job should have used with what it did use, which exposes waste early.
  - h: Reserve stock for approved jobs
    p:
      - Reservations stop two teams promising the same inverter to different customers. Reserved stock stays in the store but is no longer available to others.
  - h: Set reorder levels
    p:
      - For each item you stock regularly, choose a level at which you need to reorder, based on lead time and usage. An alert at that level beats a phone call from site.
  - h: Count on a schedule
    p:
      - Count a section of the store each week rather than everything once a year. Record the difference and the reason, so the ledger and the shelf agree.
takeaways:
  - One catalogue, named stores, a reason for every movement.
  - Issues tie to jobs, reservations protect approved jobs.
  - Reorder levels and regular counts keep the ledger honest.
